    <!-- A Super 32X game with two sound systems. The Mega Drive side (unit omitted): the game's sound driver (0xE000-0xFE68 in the file) runs on the 68000
         and sends the YM2612 commands to a small Z80 program (0xFE68, loaded by the game's routine 0xEF26); the sampled sounds are fed to the Z80 through a ring buffer.
         The driver calls routines that the game keeps in its RAM (the first 0x404F bytes of RAM, copied from 0x82D6E: m68k_ram_load) and talks to the master SH-2
         through the communication registers, waiting for the answer; cart_adapter="idle" answers every command at once. The game starts the driver with 0xEF26 (the
         Z80 program), 0xEE78 (reset the 16 channel slots) and 0xEEB0 (enable the sound routine, which the vertical-blank handler calls once a frame: 0xE7AE).
         A song or an effect is started by 0xEDBE with its number in D0: 1 to 10 are songs of 5 to 7 channels, 11 to 42 shorter sounds (0x0B only silences the PSG), the
         table of the numbers (0x8B90C4) ends there.
         The long sampled sounds (the music of the demo and the effects and voices of the game; unit="pwm") are played by the slave SH-2 through the PWM unit. The game's
         68000 asks for one with two commands for the master SH-2: command 0x3C (the address of the sound's descriptor from the table at 0x7EE28 in the file, 8 bytes for each number
         from 2 to 42, and its priority) and command 0x08 with the argument 12, which makes the master tell the slave to start it. The master program starts when the 68000
         writes "SH" "GO" into the first two communication words (sh2_start_words). -->
    <driver m68k_ram_load="0x082D6E:0xFF0000:0x404F" m68k_init="0x0EF26 0x0EE78 0x0EEB0" m68k_frame="0x0E7AE" requests="0x0EDBE" warmup_frames="4" region="japan" z80_reset="released" cart_adapter="idle" sh2_start_words="0x5348 0x474F" sh2_sample_table="0x7EE28" cart_window="32x" cart_window_bank="0" cart_crc32="f4e9b846"/>
